[For "black"]
[Kind "sideline"]
[Start "caro-classical 4...Bf5"]
[Name "Classical: 5.Ng5"]
5. Ng5 { Keeps the knight out of the bishop's reach, but this is its third move and it stands on an odd square. }
5... e6 { The usual ...e6. }
6. N1f3 { The most common move, and it sets up a trick: Nxf7 is in the air, with Ne5+ to follow. }
( 6. Nxf7?? { The same sacrifice a move sooner, with no knight ready to follow up. }
  6... Kxf7 7. Nf3 Nf6 { White has given a knight for a pawn, and Black's king is perfectly safe on f7 behind the pawn on e6. [%csl Gf7] } )
6... h6 { Asks the knight at once. It has only poor squares, and the sacrifice on f7 does not work. [%cal Gh6g5] }
7. Nh3 { The only safe retreat, and a bad square for a knight. }
( 7. Nxf7? { Played in most games at this level, and it fails. White gives a knight for a pawn and hopes to chase the king. }
  7... Kxf7 8. Ne5+ Ke8 { The king steps back and nothing happens: White has a knight less for a pawn, and the king is safe. [%csl Ge8] } )
7... Nf6 { Develops. White's knights have spent six moves getting to f3 and h3, and Black is already better. } *
